ok   connect and login
ok   transcript: the greeting, USER, PASS (the password is not in it), FEAT, TYPE
ok   PWD is the root
ok   CWD, PWD, CDUP
ok   a CWD to a file or above the root
ok   listEntries (MLSD): the entries of the root
ok   ... with dates (the file was made a moment ago)
ok   listEntries of a subdirectory: names with blanks and UTF-8
ok   LIST: ls -l lines, the file names at the end
ok   NLST: the names, one per line
ok   listEntries without MLSD (LIST, ls -l format) gives the same entries
ok   a directory that does not exist: null, 550
ok   an empty directory: an empty list
ok   a directory with 1500 files (about 100 KB of listing)
ok   retrieveAll: a small file
ok   retrieveAll: an empty file
ok   retrieveAll: every byte value
ok   retrieveAll: 1.5 MB
ok   retrieveAll: over the limit fails (the connection stays usable)
ok   retrieveAll: a file that does not exist: null, 550, the connection is fine
ok   retrieveAll: a directory is refused
ok   download to a file: 1.5 MB
ok   ... the file is identical
ok   download with resume: the rest is added (REST 700000)
ok   ... and the file is whole
ok   download with resume of a file that is complete: nothing is sent, success
ok   download with resume of a local file that is longer than the remote one: REST is refused (554)
ok   store: a small file
ok   store: an empty file
ok   store: every byte value
ok   store: replaces a file that is there
ok   append: adds to it
ok   append to a file that is not there makes it
ok   store: 300000 bytes (the limit of the server is 400000)
ok   store: over the limit: 552 and the connection stays usable
ok   store: into a directory that does not exist, over a directory
ok   upload from a file: 1 MB would go over the limit (552); the part that came stays
ok   upload from a file: 300000 bytes
ok   upload with resume: the server has 100000 bytes (SIZE), the rest is sent after REST
ok   upload of a local file that does not exist
ok   TYPE A
ok   ASCII download: LF on the disk is CRLF on the wire and LF again here (a lone CR stays, CRLF on disk becomes LF)
ok   ASCII upload: LF here is CRLF on the wire and LF on the disk
ok   TYPE I again
ok   ASCII on the wire: every line ends with CRLF, CRLF on the disk stays CRLF, a lone CR stays
ok   MKD, RNFR / RNTO, RMD
ok   MKD of a name with a quote in it: the reply doubles the quote
ok   DELE
ok   RMD of a directory with something in it, of a file, of the root
ok   RENAME of a file that is not there, onto a name that exists
ok   SIZE and MDTM
ok   a path with CR or LF is refused by the client, nothing is sent
ok   after quit
ok   a user without write rights: reads and lists
ok   ... cannot store, append, delete, make or remove a directory, rename
ok   ... and nothing changed
ok   a wrong password: connect fails (530), the client is closed
ok   EPSV: list, download, upload of 100000 bytes, and the command on the wire
ok   PASV: list, download, upload of 100000 bytes, and the command on the wire
ok   EPRT: list, download, upload of 100000 bytes, and the command on the wire
ok   PORT: list, download, upload of 100000 bytes, and the command on the wire
ok   URLs: other schemes, no host, bad ports
ok   a URL with a password and no TLS: refused, the password is never sent (AUTH TLS is required for it)
ok   ... with authTls NEVER in the options the URL works
ok   a URL without credentials logs in as anonymous (the server has no anonymous account: 530)
ok   nothing listens there: no client, a reason
ok   the scripted server: login
ok   a PASV reply with a foreign address (10.255.255.1): the client goes to the address of the control connection, the data arrives
ok   EPSV answered with 500: PASV is used instead (and EPSV is not tried again)
ok   a greeting 120 is followed by the 220
ok   a greeting 421: connect fails
ok   a data connection that stays silent: the client gives up after dataTimeoutMs
ok   a listing that never ends is cut at maxList (the error says so), memory stays small
ok   a control connection cut after EPSV: the transfer fails, the client is closed
ok   a reply to EPSV that cannot be read
ok   a PWD reply without a quoted path
ok   a login that is refused: the text of the server is in the error
